Physics-Constrained LLM-Driven Discovery of Control Theorems for Cryogenic Propellant Management

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Version: 0.4 

AI : chatGpt, Claude, Gemini, Deepseek 

Abstract

Future lunar and Martian transportation architectures require long-duration storage, transfer, and conservation of cryogenic propellants. Cryogenic Fluid Management (CFM) is an enabling technology whose control challenges are compounded by nonlinear thermodynamics, two-phase flow, heat transfer, and slosh dynamics. This proposal develops a Physics-Constrained LLM-Driven Discovery Framework (PCLDF) that systematically transforms scientific knowledge into mathematically testable models, candidate theorems, and formally verified control algorithms. The central hypothesis is that large language models (LLMs), embedded within a constrained workflow where physical laws, symbolic computation, and formal verification act as independent validators, can accelerate the generation of mathematically grounded engineering solutions. The framework treats LLMs as hypothesis-generation engines, not as sources of truth. The primary application is single-tank cryogenic pressure control under heat leak, formalized as an Irreversible Port-Hamiltonian System (IPHS) with hybrid discrete-continuous dynamics. The expected contributions are: (i) a reproducible methodology for converting scientific knowledge into candidate mathematical and algorithmic innovations with formal guarantees, (ii) a formally certified robust Model Predictive Control (MPC) architecture with Control Barrier Function (CBF) safety filters for cryogenic pressure control, and (iii) a novel integration of LLM-driven structural proposal with existing formal verification stacks (SOS, SMT, CEGIS, KeYmaera X). The thesis is explicitly theoretical: all validation is performed via symbolic mathematics, formal proof, and high-fidelity numerical simulation.

Keywords: LLM-assisted discovery, cryogenic propellant management, theorem synthesis, Lyapunov functions, control barrier functions, Port-Hamiltonian systems, exergy, model predictive control, tube-based robust MPC, reduced-order modeling, formal verification, sum-of-squares, differential dynamic logic, hybrid systems.

