1. Introduction and Motivation

1.1 The Traditional Engineering Workflow and Its Augmentation

The conventional engineering workflow is linear:

\text{Problem} \rightarrow \text{Model} \rightarrow \text{Simulation} \rightarrow \text{Design} \rightarrow \text{Validation}.

This research investigates an upstream augmentation:

\boxed{\text{Scientific Knowledge} \rightarrow \text{Problem Discovery} \rightarrow \text{Mathematical Hypothesis} \rightarrow \text{Theorem / Certificate} \rightarrow \text{Algorithm} \rightarrow \text{Simulation} \rightarrow \text{Formal Verification}}.

The motivation is not to replace engineers or physicists with LLMs, but to explore whether LLMs can accelerate exploration of the enormous space of mathematical formulations lying between established physical laws and engineering solutions. This question is particularly relevant to complex space systems where critical problems arise at the interfaces between thermodynamics, fluid mechanics, control theory, and optimization.

1.2 Engineering Motivation: Cryogenic Fluid Management

Cryogenic Fluid Management (CFM) concerns storage, transfer, measurement, and control of ultra-cold fluids such as liquid oxygen (LOX), liquid hydrogen (LH₂), and liquid methane (LCH₄). NASA identifies CFM as an integral technology for Earth-to-orbit transportation and human missions to the Moon and Mars. Long-duration cryogenic storage presents a fundamental difficulty: environmental heat leak Q_{\text{env}} > 0 increases propellant internal energy, causing liquid-to-vapor phase change and a rise in tank pressure. Traditional pressure management may require venting, which leads to propellant mass loss. NASA therefore investigates zero-boil-off (ZBO) and reduced-boil-off (RBO) approaches based on active thermal management and fluid mixing.

Recent reviews explicitly identify the gap this thesis addresses. The npj Microgravity review “Cryogenic propellant management in space: open challenges and perspectives” (2024) and the Energies systematic review “Energy Dissipation and Efficiency Challenges of Cryogenic Sloshing in Aerospace Propellant Tanks” (2025) both call out the need for physics-informed reduced-order models that preserve nonlinear coupling between slosh, heat transfer, and phase change while remaining tractable for control design. This proposal directly targets that acknowledged open problem.

1.3 Primary Case Study: Single-Tank Pressure Control

While the ultimate goal is autonomous cryogenic propellant management, the thesis focuses on a well-defined sub-problem that is both physically rich and mathematically tractable:

Single-tank pressure control of a cryogenic propellant (LOX or LH₂) under continuous environmental heat leak, using active cooling and pressurant injection, with slosh dynamics treated as a bounded disturbance.

This sub-problem is selected for the following reasons:

· It is a genuine CFM challenge with mission relevance (zero-boil-off pressure control).
· It involves coupled nonlinear thermodynamics and phase change, so the mathematical structure is nontrivial.
· It is sufficiently narrow that formal verification of control certificates (Lyapunov functions, barrier functions) is feasible within a PhD timeline.
· It avoids the added complexity of transfer-line chilldown, two-tank interactions, and microgravity fluid configuration, which can be treated as extensions.

SpaceX's March 2024 Starship demonstration of intra-vehicle tank-to-tank liquid oxygen transfer (header tank to main tank) is acknowledged as an important step, but it is not the problem addressed here. Ship-to-ship transfer remains NET late 2026 and involves additional challenges (docking, settling, long transfer lines) that are beyond the scope of this thesis. The single-tank problem is a prerequisite for any transfer architecture.

1.4 Theoretical Nature of the Thesis

This is a theoretical doctoral thesis. No physical experiments will be performed by the author. All validation is conducted through:

1. Symbolic computation (SymPy, Mathematica) for algebraic verification.
2. Formal proof assistants where feasible (Lean, Coq, KeYmaera X) for simplified and hybrid systems.
3. Sum-of-squares (SOS) programming and SMT solvers (dReal, Z3) for Lyapunov/barrier certificate verification.
4. High-fidelity numerical simulation (CFD or nodal codes) used as a surrogate for physical reality, anchored to publicly available NASA benchmark datasets (MHTB, K-Site).

The absence of experimental data is a deliberate choice: the contribution is methodological and mathematical. The thesis demonstrates that, for a well-defined physical system, an LLM-driven pipeline can produce provably correct control theorems and algorithms, validated entirely in silico.

