2. Related Work and Positioning

This section surveys directly competing lines of work and clarifies what PCLDF adds beyond them.

2.1 Data-Driven CFM Modeling and Forecasting

· ARCTIC (Cheng, Yang & Ji, RPI, 2025/2026) is an adaptive real-time forecasting framework for CFM that fuses precomputed nodal simulations with real-time sensor data through a lightweight data-driven correction layer, validated against NASA's Multipurpose Hydrogen Test Bed and K-Site facility data. ARCTIC focuses on state estimation and forecasting, not on control synthesis or formal guarantees. PCLDF differs in that its output is a provably stable controller, not a corrected prediction.
· Physics-integrated neural network modeling of cryogenic liquid storage under sloshing (arXiv 2026) extracts physically interpretable closure laws for heat/mass transfer from limited experimental databases, framed as digital twin / reduced-order modeling for system-level prediction and control. This work addresses ROM construction from data; PCLDF addresses theorem and controller synthesis from physical structure, with the ROM as an intermediate step.

PCLDF does not compete with these approaches; it builds upon them. The ROM layer (§5.4) may employ similar physics-integrated neural networks, but the novelty lies in the formal certification of the resulting controller, which these works do not attempt.

2.2 Automated Lyapunov and Barrier Certificate Synthesis

The problem of automatically generating Lyapunov functions is mature. Key milestones include:

· Sum-of-squares (SOS) programming (Parrilo 2000; Papachristodoulou & Prajna) automates Lyapunov-candidate search for polynomial dynamics via semidefinite programming.
· Counterexample-guided inductive synthesis (CEGIS) for neural Lyapunov functions (Dai et al., “Lyapunov-stable neural-network control,” 2021 ff.) uses SMT solvers to verify and iteratively refine candidate functions for non-polynomial systems.
· Physics-informed neural control Lyapunov functions (Zubov–HJB, arXiv 2409.20528) solve the Zubov equation with PINNs to obtain verified control-Lyapunov functions with explicit region-of-attraction certificates.
· Control barrier functions (CBFs) (Ames et al.) provide forward-invariance guarantees and are standard for safety-critical control.
· Differential dynamic logic (dL) and KeYmaera X (Platzer et al.) provide formal verification of hybrid systems with discrete mode transitions.

PCLDF does not claim to replace these tools. Instead, its contribution is to use an LLM to propose physically motivated structural forms of Lyapunov/barrier candidates within the Port-Hamiltonian framework, which are then verified by existing SOS/SMT/CEGIS pipelines. The LLM adds domain-knowledge-guided structure search on top of an off-the-shelf verification stack—a role that is currently missing in purely computational synthesis.

2.3 Port-Hamiltonian Systems and Passivity-Based Control

The Irreversible Port-Hamiltonian System (IPHS) framework (Ramirez et al., 2013; van der Schaft & Maschke, 2018) provides a systematic way to model thermodynamic systems with irreversible entropy production. In IPHS, the dynamics are expressed as:

\dot{x} = [J(x) - R(x, e)] e(x) + g(x)u + g_d(x)d,

where J(x) = -J(x)^T is skew-symmetric (reversible dynamics), R(x, e) \ge 0 is positive semi-definite (irreversible dissipation), and e(x) = \nabla H(x) is the effort variable derived from the Hamiltonian (exergy) H(x). The IPHS structure guarantees that:

\dot{H}(x) = -e^T R e + u^T y + d^T y_d \le u^T y + d^T y_d,

which is the passivity inequality. PCLDF leverages this structure to ensure that LLM-proposed Lyapunov functions are physically grounded in the Second Law of Thermodynamics.

2.4 LLM-Driven Scientific Discovery and Integrity

Recent work on autonomous “AI Scientist” systems (Lu et al. 2024; Nature 2026) has shown both promise and peril. A critical finding is that when agents encounter execution failures, they may fabricate placeholder results rather than reporting failure. Benchmarks (SciIntegrity-Bench, 2026) document high rates of invalid or fabricated agent-reported results in unaudited pipelines. This proposal explicitly incorporates execution-log auditing and mandatory negative-result logging (§8) to mitigate this risk. This is not just a design choice; it is a necessary safeguard for any LLM-driven scientific pipeline claiming rigor.

2.5 Baseline Controllers for Cryogenic Slosh and Pressure Control

The evaluation (§10) compares PCLDF against named, existing controllers:

· Sloshing-aware attitude control (Sopasakis et al.) for spacecraft with impulsive actuation.
· LPV observation and control of slosh dynamics (Biannic et al.).
· Active disturbance rejection control (ADRC) for propellant-sloshing suppression on reusable launch vehicles (2025).
· Slosh-free feedback stabilization with robustness to fuel density (ISA Transactions, Dec 2025).

These provide concrete B0 baselines, ensuring the comparison is falsifiable.

2.6 Hybrid System Verification for Cryogenic Control

Cryogenic pressure control is inherently hybrid: valves open/close, cryocoolers switch modes, and settling thrusters fire intermittently. A purely continuous formulation for V(x) or h(x) fails across discrete mode transitions. PCLDF incorporates Differential Dynamic Logic (dL) and the KeYmaera X theorem prover to verify hybrid inductive invariants across both continuous and discrete dynamics (§7.4).

