3. Central Research Question and Hypotheses

3.1 Central Research Question

Can a physics-constrained LLM architecture, coupled with symbolic mathematics, formal verification, and high-fidelity simulation, systematically transform established scientific knowledge into provably correct reduced-order models, stability/robustness certificates, and control algorithms for a single cryogenic propellant tank?

This leads to three subsidiary questions, reflecting the reduced scope:

· RQ1 (Model Synthesis): Can LLM agents construct a reduced-order IPHS model from independently established physical laws that preserves the essential nonlinear coupling between thermodynamics, phase change, and slosh, with a bounded approximation error?
· RQ2 (Theorem Discovery): Can the system propose and formally verify Lyapunov functions, control barrier functions, or robustness certificates for the reduced-order model, using existing SOS/SMT/CEGIS/KeYmaera X tools?
· RQ3 (Algorithm Synthesis): Can the verified certificates be integrated into a tube-based robust MPC with CBF safety filter that guarantees stability and constraint satisfaction in simulation?

3.2 Central Hypothesis

A constrained LLM-based discovery architecture, in which the LLM proposes structural forms of IPHS models and certificates that are verified by independent symbolic and formal tools, can accelerate the generation of provably correct control algorithms for a cryogenic propellant tank, without delegating physical or mathematical truth to the language model.

This hypothesis deliberately avoids the claim that LLMs independently discover new physical laws. The LLM is a proposal engine; the formal tools are the arbiters of truth.

