5. Reduced-Order Modeling (ROM)

5.1 ROM Construction

The full IPHS model (Section 4.4) has 11 states. For control design, a reduced-order model (ROM) is required that preserves the essential nonlinear coupling while being tractable for real-time MPC.

The ROM is constructed via timescale separation and aggregation:

· Fast dynamics: Slosh mode (\eta, p_\eta) evolves on timescales of seconds (slosh frequency \omega_n \sim 1–5 rad/s).
· Intermediate dynamics: Interfacial boundary layer (S_I, V_I, N_I) evolves on timescales of minutes (phase change).
· Slow dynamics: Bulk liquid and ullage vapor (S_L, V_L, N_L, S_V, V_V, N_V) evolve on timescales of hours (thermal stratification, boil-off).

For pressure control, the relevant slow dynamics are the bulk liquid and vapor states. The ROM state is:

z = \left[ P, \; T_L, \; T_V, \; m_L, \; m_V \right]^T \in \mathbb{R}^5,

where P is ullage pressure, T_L, T_V are bulk temperatures, and m_L, m_V are masses.

5.2 ROM Error Bound

The ROM error is defined as:

e(t) = x(t) - \Pi(z(t)),

where \Pi: \mathbb{R}^5 \to \mathbb{R}^{11} is an embedding map. From the IPHS structure and the timescale separation, the following error bound is established:

Theorem 5.1 (ROM Error Bound). Under the assumption that the slosh dynamics are bounded by \|\eta\| \le \eta_{\max} and the interfacial boundary layer is quasi-static, the ROM error satisfies:

\| e(t) \| \le \epsilon_{\text{ROM}} := \frac{\eta_{\max} \cdot C_{\text{coupling}} + d_{\max}}{\lambda_{\min}(R_{\text{slow}})},

where C_{\text{coupling}} is a constant depending on the slosh-thermal coupling coefficients (\gamma_1, \gamma_2, \beta_1, \beta_2), d_{\max} is the disturbance bound, and \lambda_{\min}(R_{\text{slow}}) is the minimum eigenvalue of the slow subsystem's dissipation matrix.

Proof Sketch. The error dynamics satisfy:

\dot{e} = [J_{\text{slow}} - R_{\text{slow}}] e + \Delta_{\text{coupling}}(\eta, d),

where \Delta_{\text{coupling}} captures the neglected slosh-thermal coupling and disturbances. Using the passivity of the slow subsystem and the boundedness of \eta and d, the error is uniformly bounded. \square

This error bound \epsilon_{\text{ROM}} is used in the tube-based robust MPC design (§7.2) to guarantee constraint satisfaction despite model mismatch.

5.3 Physics-Integrated Neural Network ROM (Optional)

An alternative ROM approach uses physics-integrated neural networks (PINNs) trained on high-fidelity CFD data from NASA MHTB and K-Site datasets. The PINN preserves the IPHS structure by parameterizing the Hamiltonian H_\theta(x) and dissipation R_\theta(x, e) with neural networks, ensuring passivity by construction. This approach is included as an optional extension if timescale separation proves insufficient.

