6. Theorem Discovery Layer

This section describes the LLM-driven theorem discovery pipeline, which operates on the IPHS structure and is verified by existing formal tools.

6.1 LLM's Role: Structural Form Proposal

For the cryogenic tank system, the LLM proposes physically motivated structural forms of Lyapunov/barrier candidates within the IPHS framework. The LLM does not perform arbitrary function search; instead, it operates on the structure of the interconnection matrix J(x) and dissipation matrix R(x, e).

Candidate Lyapunov Function Family. The LLM proposes candidates of the form:

V(x) = A(x) + W_{\text{slosh}}(\eta, p_\eta),

where A(x) is the exergy function (Section 4.2) and W_{\text{slosh}} is a positive-definite slosh energy term. The LLM parameterizes the weight matrices:

V(x) = H(x) + \frac{1}{2} \begin{bmatrix} \eta \\ p_\eta \end{bmatrix}^T \begin{bmatrix} q_{11} & q_{12} \\ q_{12} & q_{22} \end{bmatrix} \begin{bmatrix} \eta \\ p_\eta \end{bmatrix},

with q_{11}, q_{22} > 0 and q_{11} q_{22} - q_{12}^2 > 0 (positive definiteness).

Candidate Control Barrier Function. The LLM proposes CBF candidates of the form:

h(x) = P_{\max} - P(x) + \phi(T_L, T_V),

where \phi is a correction term accounting for temperature-dependent saturation pressure. The LLM proposes \phi based on the Clausius-Clapeyron relation:

\phi(T_L, T_V) = c_1 (T_L - T_{\text{ref}}) + c_2 (T_V - T_{\text{ref}}),

with c_1, c_2 to be determined.

