9. Tube-Based Robust MPC Integration with Formal ROM Error

To bridge the gap between Reduced-Order Model (ROM) error and closed-loop safety guarantees, formalize the Tube-Based Robust MPC framework.

9.1 Problem Formulation

Let x(t) be the state of the high-fidelity system (full IPHS model) and z(t) be the state of the ROM:

\dot{x} = f(x, u, d), \quad \dot{z} = g(z, u), \quad \|x(0) - z(0)\| \le e_0.

From Theorem 5.1, we establish a validated ROM error bound:

\|f(z + e, u, d) - g(z, u)\| \le L_e \|e\| + \epsilon_{\text{ROM}} + d_{\max}.

9.2 Ancillary Control Law

Define the Ancillary Control Law:

u(t) = v(t) + K (x(t) - z(t)),

where v(t) is the nominal control trajectory computed by the MPC over the ROM, and K is a feedback gain matrix designed via the LLM-discovered Lyapunov function V(e) = e^T P_e e.

9.3 Robust Tube Boundary

```
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
Robust Tube Boundary S_tube(μ)
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│     High-Fidelity State Trajectory x(t)                 │
│        •───────\───────────/──────•                     │
│                \   ROM z(t)  /                          │
│                 •••••••••••••••\─────────────/•••••••••••│
│                       Nominal Trajectory                │
└─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
```

Safety Guarantee. The invariant tube size \mu(\epsilon_{\text{ROM}}, d_{\max}) is computed directly from the verified Lyapunov dissipation rate:

\mathbb{S}_{\text{tube}} = \left\{ e \in \mathbb{R}^n : e^T P_e e \le \frac{\epsilon_{\text{ROM}} + d_{\max}}{\alpha_{\min}} \right\},

where \alpha_{\min} is the minimum dissipation rate from the Lyapunov certificate (T1). The safety constraint in the MPC is tightened by the tube radius:

P_{\text{nominal}}(t) \le P_{\max} - \sqrt{\frac{\mu}{P_{e, \text{pressure}}}},

where P_{e, \text{pressure}} is the pressure component of the Lyapunov matrix P_e.

9.4 MPC Optimization Problem

At each time step, the MPC solves:

v^*_{t:t+N} = \arg\min_{v} \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \ell(z_k, v_k) + V_f(z_N),

subject to:

z_{k+1} = g(z_k, v_k),

z_k \in \mathcal{Z}_{\text{tightened}}, \quad v_k \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{tightened}},

z_N \in \mathcal{Z}_f,

where \mathcal{Z}_{\text{tightened}} = \mathcal{Z} \ominus \mathbb{S}_{\text{tube}}, V_f is a terminal cost (derived from the Lyapunov function), and \mathcal{Z}_f is the terminal set.

9.5 CBF Safety Filter

The CBF-QP safety filter solves:

u_{\text{safe}} = \arg\min_{u \in \mathcal{U}} \|u - u_{\text{MPC}}\|^2,

\text{s.t.} \quad \dot{h}(x, u) + \gamma h(x) \ge 0,

where h(x) = P_{\max} - P(x) and \gamma > 0 is a design parameter. The CBF condition is verified by the formal tools (T3).

9.6 Chain of Guarantees

The complete chain of guarantees is:

1. T4 (ROM error bound): \|x - \Pi(z)\| \le \epsilon_{\text{ROM}}.
2. T1 (Lyapunov stability): \dot{V}(e) \le -\alpha V(e) for the error dynamics.
3. Tube invariance: x(t) \in \Pi(z(t)) \oplus \mathbb{S}_{\text{tube}} for all t.
4. T3 (CBF safety): P(t) \le P_{\max} for all t.
5. T6 (Hybrid invariance): The safe set is maintained across all discrete mode transitions.

