A.3 Proof Sketch of Theorem 6.1 (Asymptotic Stability of Pressure Control)

Theorem A.3. Consider the IPHS model with the feedback law Q_{\text{cool}} = -k_P (P - P_{\text{ref}}) and \dot{N}_{\text{press}} = 0. Under the condition that the environmental heat leak Q_{\text{env}} is bounded and k_P > 0 is sufficiently large, the equilibrium pressure P = P_{\text{ref}} is asymptotically stable in the sense of Lyapunov, with Lyapunov function:

V(x) = H(x) + \frac{1}{2} \lambda_P (P - P_{\text{ref}})^2,

where \lambda_P > 0.

Proof Sketch.
We provide the key steps; the complete formal proof is carried out in the thesis using SOS/SMT tools.

1. Model Reduction. For pressure control, the dominant slow dynamics are those of the ullage vapor and the liquid-vapor interface. We ignore the slosh dynamics (assume \eta = 0) for this theorem; robustness to slosh is handled by the tube MPC.
2. Pressure Dynamics. From the IPHS equations, the pressure in the ullage P = P_V evolves according to:

\dot{P} = \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial S_V} \right) \dot{S}_V + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial V_V} \right) \dot{V}_V + \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial N_V} \right) \dot{N}_V.

For an ideal gas vapor, P = N_V R T_V / V_V, so the partial derivatives are known. Substituting the expressions for \dot{S}_V, \dot{V}_V, \dot{N}_V from the IPHS, we obtain a relation of the form:

\dot{P} = -\kappa (P - P_{\text{eq}}(T_L, Q_{\text{env}})) + \text{terms involving } Q_{\text{cool}},

where P_{\text{eq}} is the equilibrium pressure determined by heat leak and liquid temperature.

3. Lyapunov Function Construction. Let V(x) = H(x) + \frac{1}{2} \lambda_P (P - P_{\text{ref}})^2. Using the passivity property (Theorem A.1), we have:

\dot{H} \le -T_0 \sigma_s + u^T y + d^T y_d.

With u = Q_{\text{cool}} = -k_P (P - P_{\text{ref}}), the control input appears only in the first component of u (cooling power). The output y_1 = -\frac{T_L - T_0}{T_L} \approx 0 near equilibrium (small temperature difference). So the direct passivity term u^T y is small. The pressure penalty term contributes:

\lambda_P (P - P_{\text{ref}}) \dot{P}.

Substituting \dot{P} and using the fact that cooling reduces pressure (i.e., \dot{P} has a negative component proportional to Q_{\text{cool}}), we find:

\lambda_P (P - P_{\text{ref}}) \dot{P} \le -\lambda_P k_P c (P - P_{\text{ref}})^2 + \lambda_P (P - P_{\text{ref}}) \cdot (\text{disturbance terms}),

where c > 0 is a constant depending on the thermodynamic derivatives.

4. Dissipation Dominance. The entropy generation term -T_0 \sigma_s provides additional negative definiteness in the temperature and phase-change directions. For sufficiently large k_P (so that the pressure dissipation dominates the disturbance input), and appropriate choice of \lambda_P, the overall derivative satisfies:

\dot{V} \le -\alpha V + \beta \|d\|^2,

for some \alpha > 0, \beta > 0. Under bounded disturbances, the system is input-to-state stable (ISS). In the absence of disturbances (d=0), asymptotic stability follows.

5. Formal Verification. The symbolic expressions for \dot{V} are checked using SOS programming (if polynomial approximations are used) or SMT solvers (dReal) to verify that \dot{V} \le -\alpha V over a bounded region of state space. The LLM proposes the structural form and initial parameter guesses; the formal tools either confirm or provide counterexamples that guide refinement. \square

