A.4 Proof of Hybrid Safety Invariance (Theorem 7.1)

Theorem A.4. The hybrid automaton \mathcal{HA} = (\mathcal{Q}, \mathcal{X}, \mathcal{U}, f, \mathcal{D}, \text{Init}, \text{Guard}) maintains the safe set \mathcal{S} = \{x : P_{\min} \le P \le P_{\max}\} for all time, provided that the CBF condition h(x) \ge 0 is satisfied in each mode and across all guard transitions.

Proof.
We use the framework of differential dynamic logic (dL) as implemented in KeYmaera X. The safety property is expressed as the dL formula:

[\mathcal{HA}] \, (P_{\min} \le P \le P_{\max}).

The hybrid automaton is modeled as a loop of discrete mode changes interleaved with continuous evolution. The proof uses a global inductive invariant \phi(x, \sigma) that is implied by the initial condition, is preserved by each continuous evolution, and is preserved by each discrete transition.

We choose \phi(x, \sigma) \equiv (P_{\min} \le P \le P_{\max}) \wedge (h_\sigma(x) \ge 0), where h_\sigma(x) is a mode-specific CBF. For each mode \sigma, we have the continuous dynamics \dot{x} = f_\sigma(x, u_\sigma), with control u_\sigma possibly generated by an MPC or a feedback law. The CBF condition in that mode is:

\dot{h}_\sigma(x, u_\sigma) + \gamma_\sigma h_\sigma(x) \ge 0.

This ensures that if h_\sigma(x(t_0)) \ge 0, then h_\sigma(x(t)) \ge 0 for all t \ge t_0 while in mode \sigma. Since h_\sigma(x) \ge 0 implies P \le P_{\max} (or P \ge P_{\min} for lower barrier), the safe set is invariant during continuous flow.

For discrete transitions, let G_{\sigma \to \sigma'} be the guard condition that triggers the switch. The invariant must satisfy:

\phi(x, \sigma) \wedge G_{\sigma \to \sigma'}(x) \Rightarrow \phi(x, \sigma').

In our cryogenic system, the guards are based on pressure thresholds (e.g., P \ge P_{\max} triggers venting; P \le P_{\text{target}} closes the vent). By design, the CBF for the new mode \sigma' is chosen to be compatible: if P is near P_{\max}, the venting mode's CBF ensures P decreases; if P is near P_{\min}, the pressurizing mode's CBF ensures P increases. Thus the transition does not violate the invariant.

The proof in KeYmaera X proceeds by induction over the loop structure, using the continuous evolution rules (dL's differential invariant rule) for each mode and the discrete jump rule for transitions. The LLM's role is to propose the invariant \phi and the specific CBFs h_\sigma; KeYmaera X automatically verifies the proof obligations. \square

