A.5 Observability of the Reduced State (Theorem T5)

Theorem A.5. Given sensor measurements of ullage pressure P and bulk temperatures T_L, T_V, the reduced state z = [P, T_L, T_V, m_L, m_V]^T is locally observable.

Proof Sketch.
We linearize the ROM dynamics about an operating point and compute the observability matrix of the linearized system. The ROM equations are:

\dot{z} = g(z, u),

with output y = [P, T_L, T_V]^T = C z, where C selects the first three states. The observability matrix O = [C; C A; C A^2; C A^3; C A^4] (where A = \partial g / \partial z at the operating point) must have full rank 5. The rank condition is satisfied because the pressure dynamics depend on mass and temperature, and the temperature dynamics depend on heat transfer and phase change, which couple all states. The nonlinear system is locally observable if the linearization is observable (in the absence of singularities). This is verified numerically over the operating range. \square

